{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is what we astronomers call a blob, or a \nsmudge, if you want to get really technical.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5920,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may not look like much from here, but \nwhat do you expect for something near the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "literal edge of the observable universe. If \nyou were there when this light was emitted,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’d A. be at the beginning of time, and B. \nbe looking at an entire galaxy containing an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enormous black hole at its heart. It’s the \nmost distant black hole we’ve semi-directly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27600,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detected. That’s cool enough on its own, but \nas an added bonus this one smudge may have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34480,
    "dDurationMs": 6229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solved the mystery of the origin of the \nsupermassive black holes in our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46200,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Introducing Abell 2744. It’s a giant cluster \nof galaxies around 4 billion light years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away. Basically our back yard, \ncosmologically speaking. The most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57080,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interesting thing about Abel 2744—at least for \nus today—isn’t what’s in the cluster itself,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63440,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but what’s behind it. The cluster lives \nin a vast halo of dark matter that warps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69320,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space across its 4-million-light year span. \nLight from distant objects traveling through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74240,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cluster is deflected and magnified. The \ncluster is a stupendous gravitational lens that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enables us to see much further than our puny \nhuman-made telescopes would normally allow. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86840,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s why the Hubble Space Telescope studied Abel \n2744 in detail, and why it was one of the early  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94040,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must-dos for the James Webb Space Telescope. \nAnd this is where our smudge got interesting.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101240,
    "dDurationMs": 8920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JWST observes deep into infrared wavelengths, and \nshowed that the object has IR colours consistent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110160,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a galaxy whose normally visible light has \nbeen stretched out by the expanding universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116960,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increasing or redshifting its wavelength by a \nfactor of over 10. Higher redshift means larger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123880,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance, and a redshift of 10.1 means this light \nhas been traveling for around 13.2 billion years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coming to us from a time near the beginning of \ntime, when the universe was less than 3.5% its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current age. That earned our smudge the name \nUHZ1, for ultra-high redshift galaxy number  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145400,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one—where the z is the symbol for redshift.\nThat’s cool, but JWST has now found galaxies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quite a bit more distant than this. The really \nexciting moment came when we pointed another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159120,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbiting satellite at the smudge. This is the \nimage by the Chandra X-ray telescope. It turns  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166160,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out there are a LOT of X-rays coming from this \nlittle blob. Now X-rays are much harder to bring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172960,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a sharp focus so the 4 pixels of X-ray light \naren’t coming from across this spread-out region,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179880,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but rather from somewhere inside this region.\nThere’s really only one known way for a galaxy to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186520,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be blasting out X-rays at this level, and that’s \nby harboring a quasar. To refresh your memory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193280,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a quasar is when the supermassive black \nhole—the SMBH—that lives at the center of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199880,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every galaxy starts feeding. As matter swirls \ntowards the black hole it’s superheated until  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207240,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it outshines the entire surrounding galaxy. \nAnd just before it reaches the black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conditions get so crazy that the space outside \nthe black hole glows bright with high-energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217760,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "X-rays. Based on the amount of X-ray light and \nthe distance, we can estimate that the black hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223000,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must be 40 million times the Sun’s mass. That’s \n10 times the mass of the Milky Way’s own SMBH. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230600,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UHZ1 is the most distant quasar ever \ndiscovered, which also makes it the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235920,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earliest known quasar and so the earliest \nblack hole that we actually have evidence for."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So with that out of the way, let’s get to the \ngood stuff. To understand why UHZ1 is so exciting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have to understand one of the biggest debates \nin cosmology - the origin of supermassive black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253560,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes. Essentially every galaxy in the modern \nuniverse has at its core a black hole that’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259840,
    "dDurationMs": 6790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "100,000 to several billion times the mass of \nthe Sun. We also see much smaller black holes—so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266630,
    "dDurationMs": 7290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called stellar black holes—that can be in the \nrough range of 10 to 100 solar masses. Oddly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273920,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we don’t see black holes in the middle range of \n100 to 100,000. We’ll come back to why that’s odd."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281680,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, we know how to make stellar \nblack holes—they’re what you get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285600,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when the core of a massive star collapses \non itself after the star dies. So how do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you make a supermassive black holes \ngrew? That’s the big question. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294840,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could be they grew from the very first \nstellar corpses in the early universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299760,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gulping down gas and merging with other black \nholes for billions of years to reach their current  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305080,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enormous sizes. But over the last decade or so, \nas we looked to greater and greater distances,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311960,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we started to find quasars shining out from the \nfirst billion years of cosmic time—quasars powered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by supermassive black holes that should \nnot have had enough time to get that big."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two potential solutions to this \nconundrum. Either the black holes started  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328720,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small and grew way, way faster than we thought \nthey could. Or they started much bigger than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335160,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black holes that form in stellar deaths in \nthe modern universe. These are the small seed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340080,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and heavy seed models respectively. UHZ1 \nis going to help us choose between them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346120,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with the small seed model. Can a \nstellar corpse grow into a quasar engine in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less than a billion years? Well, it’s tricky. \nThere’s a limit to how fast a black hole can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358080,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feed even with an endless supply of gas. As \ngas spiraling into a black hole heats up,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364040,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it blasts out radiation which pushes \non the infalling gas and counters the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368280,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole’s gravity. Bigger black \nholes can eat more and radiate more,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372440,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but there’s always an approximate upper limit \nthat increases with that mass—it’s called the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eddington limit. You can calculate how big a \nblack hole would grow feeding at this maximum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383600,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rate for a billion years—and the a nswer is … \nnot big enough to explain those early quasars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, there are various tricks we can \ninclude to make the small seed model work.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are scenarios in which black holes \ncan feed faster than the Eddington limit,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although they still need to feed non-stop to \nreach supermassive status so quickly—which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself is a problem. Or you can form a lot of \nblack holes close to each other and have them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "merge very quickly. Or you can start out with \nreally, really big stars leaving behind black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417000,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes that are maybe 100 or even 1000 times larger \nthan are produced today. In the modern universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423760,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all the heavy elements released in past supernovae \ncause gas clouds to fragment as they collapse,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leading to smaller stars. But the \npristine hydrogen and helium that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "filled the universe at the beginning \nheld together as it collapsed, probably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439040,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leading to gigantic stars and commensurately \nmassive, but still not supermassive black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With a combination of these tricks—fast and \nconsistent feeding and biggish stellar black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452920,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole seeds—it’s possible to make supermassive \nblack holes in under a billion years. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still doesn’t explain why we don’t see any \nblack holes in the 100 to 100,000 solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463200,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass range. If SMBHs grew from stellar black \nholes by passing through this range then you’d  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469560,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expect some stragglers to still be in that \nrange, but that doesn’t seem to be the case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so let’s try the heavy seed model. What if \nthe seeds of supermassive black holes weren’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483080,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even born from the hyper-dense cores of dead \nstars? In fact it's actually a misconception that you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490520,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need extremely dense matter to make a black hole. \nFor any given mass, there’s a particular size that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496800,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass needs to be crushed into in order for it \nto form a black hole. This is the Schwarzschild  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502600,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radius. For the Sun, it’s around 3 km, and a \nsolar mass crammed within that radius would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have the same density as a typical 3000 meter \ntall mountain crammed into a cubic centimeter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515560,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, a typical chonky \nsupermassive black hole of a billion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520480,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solar masses has a Schwarzschild radius \nthe size of Neptune’s orbit. A billion suns  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crammed into that region would have the \ndensity of … cotton candy. That’s right,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fill a solar system with cotton candy and \nyou’d get an instant giant black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The early universe did not contain \nsignificant quantities of cotton candy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542560,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as far as we can tell anyway. But it \nwas filled with a lot of gas that would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546680,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "later form stars and galaxies. As that gas \nstarted to collapse to make those things,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551840,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the resulting protogalactic clouds may have \nhad cores dense enough to collapse directly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557800,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into black holes without ever forming a star. \nAgain, this only works in the early universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where the unpolluted hydrogen and helium \ndoesn’t tend to fragment as it collapses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570760,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And as it happens, theoretical calculations \nof the expected sizes of these direct collapse  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes are right around 100,000 \nsolar mass mark. That gives us a way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582720,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "go straight from gas to the smallest SMBHs \nwithout having to grow from stellar seeds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589680,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the intermediate range. And once you \nhave a small SMBH, a pretty sensible rate of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596440,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feeding can turn it into a large SMBH \nin mere hundreds of millions of years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602720,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, great. Between our two models - small \nversus heavy seed, it’s looking like the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607200,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heavy seed model feels more plausible. So let’s see what our new player—UHZ1—has to say about this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616080,
    "dDurationMs": 11480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Previous record holders for most distant quasar, \nlike J1342+0928, J0313–1806, and J1007+2115,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627560,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are all similarly massive, and they had already \nput the ‘small seed’ formation theory under a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634840,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lot of stress in the past few years. But they \nwere all found right around 700 million years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642240,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after the big bang—just on the boundary of the \namount of time needed to grow a small seed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assuming some very creative astrophysics \nto allow a really cosmic bulking phase."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656000,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But UHZ1 shaves 200 million years of the \nallowed growth time. If it was marginally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662120,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plausible to grow a small seed into an SMBH \nbefore, now it’s feeling nearly impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look a bit more closely at this object \nto try to make this more concrete. Based on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675040,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the JWST images, we can get an idea of the amount \nof starlight versus quasar light in this object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even those images are dominated by quasar \nlight. The galaxy has around 40 million  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689000,
    "dDurationMs": 10400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Suns worth of mass in stars, (the same as it's mass in the black hole) and that's around 1000  times less than the stars in the Milky Way. This isn’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unusual in itself. We expect galaxies from \nso long ago to still be building up mass and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704920,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "converting gas into stars. But for most of \nthe universe’s history we see a pretty tight  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "correlation between the number of stars in \na galaxy and the mass of its central black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714880,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole. That has been taken by some to indicate \nthat galaxies and their central black holes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grow together in relative lock-step. UHZ1 \nsuggests otherwise—it tells us that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727640,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole starts relatively enormous \nand then the stellar mass catches up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733680,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "UHZ1 may represent the first in a new class of \nobject called an OBG - for overly-massive black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740600,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole galaxy, or outsized black hole galaxy. OBGs \nare objects hypothesized to exist in the early  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe in which the black hole and stellar \nmasses were around the same. The amount of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752920,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infrared and X-ray light coming from UHZ1 is \nconsistent with simulations of OBG formation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so maybe this class of object is \nno longer hypothetical. If so,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that tells us a lot. OBGs only work \nif their central black hole is formed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770080,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by direct collapse, not through \nthe growth of a stellar remnant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774600,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Overall, things are looking pretty \ngood for the heavy seed model, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778200,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quite a bit less good for the small seed model.\nBut it’s not quite a smoking gun. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maybe UHZ1 is weird. Perhaps its SMBH \nformed in an unusual way—for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790480,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from a rare and massive primordial black hole or \nin an exotic environment like a quasistar. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798120,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fact that observations of UHZ1 line up so well \nwith the direct collapse and the OBG model would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805440,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tend to disfavor these alternate explanations, \nat least in UHZ1s case. And even if UHZ1 is an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OBG it doesn’t mean other supermassive black \nholes couldn’t have formed from small seeds.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in general the case for the latter as an \nimportant source of SMBHs is being whittled away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825560,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what’s next? Well, we keep \nlooking. UHZ1 is the first of its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830880,
    "dDurationMs": 7556,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kind. But in astronomy where there's one, there's often many and hopefully soon we'll find the many. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838436,
    "dDurationMs": 5284,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As JWST and Chandra continue their \ngood work, we’ll develop a clearer and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clearer picture of what the earliest times \nwere like as we discover more distant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849960,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "glimmers of the earliest stars and quasars \nthat first lit up a newly-born spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857320,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Guys. Two things before we go. First I wanted \nto tell you about fascinating fails over on PBS  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863520,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Terra. It’s a show about scientific discoveries \nthat were made by accident. Their latest episode  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870360,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is about - the story of Penzias and Wilson and \ntheir discovery of cosmic background radiation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876240,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a link in description! And if you go over, \ntell them (politely) that Space Time sent you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881480,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Next, I wanted to let you know that because you \nasked, the classic black hole orbits design is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888120,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now available on the Space Time Merch store as \na t-shirt and for the first time a sweatshirt.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894680,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's no better way to perpare for your \nnext surprise encounter with a black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898720,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then to carry a geodesic map of the \nexterior with you at all times. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903400,
    "dDurationMs": 7847,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now you can be warm at the same time. There’s \na link in the description to our Merch store!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
